1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,641
Behind the wheels of many supercars,

2
00:00:02,641 --> 00:00:05,282
the brake discs are not shiny steel.

3
00:00:05,462 --> 00:00:06,819
They are dark grey,

4
00:00:06,819 --> 00:00:09,105
with a strange speckled surface,

5
00:00:09,105 --> 00:00:11,248
and they cost more than a used

6
00:00:11,248 --> 00:00:11,534
car.

7
00:00:11,714 --> 00:00:13,733
They are made of ceramic,

8
00:00:13,733 --> 00:00:16,398
the same family of materials as a

9
00:00:16,398 --> 00:00:17,287
coffee cup.

10
00:00:17,467 --> 00:00:20,883
Today, ten parts of a car's brakes,

11
00:00:20,883 --> 00:00:23,225
and why each one exists.

12
00:00:23,576 --> 00:00:26,048
Brakes have one job that sounds simple.

13
00:00:26,228 --> 00:00:28,564
Turn the speed of the car into

14
00:00:28,564 --> 00:00:30,977
heat, and get rid of that heat.

15
00:00:31,157 --> 00:00:33,712
A heavy car stopping from high speed

16
00:00:33,712 --> 00:00:36,410
produces enough heat to make the discs

17
00:00:36,410 --> 00:00:36,765
glow.

18
00:00:36,945 --> 00:00:39,749
Every clever part of a modern brake

19
00:00:39,749 --> 00:00:41,992
is about handling that heat,

20
00:00:41,992 --> 00:00:43,595
without losing grip,

21
00:00:43,595 --> 00:00:45,037
weight or control.

22
00:00:45,387 --> 00:00:48,422
As always, every fact here comes from

23
00:00:48,422 --> 00:00:49,653
a named source,

24
00:00:49,653 --> 00:00:50,883
the car makers,

25
00:00:50,883 --> 00:00:54,083
brake makers and automotive references,

26
00:00:54,083 --> 00:00:56,544
all listed in the description.

27
00:00:56,724 --> 00:00:58,953
Let's start with the ceramic disc.

28
00:00:59,303 --> 00:01:02,390
Why are supercar brake discs made of

29
00:01:02,390 --> 00:01:03,076
ceramic?

30
00:01:03,256 --> 00:01:06,196
Because they handle heat better and weigh

31
00:01:06,196 --> 00:01:06,913
much less.

32
00:01:07,093 --> 00:01:09,951
A carbon ceramic disc is made of

33
00:01:09,951 --> 00:01:13,792
carbon fibres mixed into a ceramic material

34
00:01:13,792 --> 00:01:15,847
called silicon carbide.

35
00:01:16,027 --> 00:01:19,129
It stays strong at temperatures that would

36
00:01:19,129 --> 00:01:21,493
make an iron disc lose its grip,

37
00:01:21,493 --> 00:01:23,857
and it weighs about half as much

38
00:01:23,857 --> 00:01:25,851
as an iron disc of the same

39
00:01:25,851 --> 00:01:26,220
size.

40
00:01:26,571 --> 00:01:28,950
When iron brakes get too hot after

41
00:01:28,950 --> 00:01:30,349
repeated hard stops,

42
00:01:30,349 --> 00:01:31,679
they start to fade.

43
00:01:31,859 --> 00:01:34,101
The pedal goes soft and the car

44
00:01:34,101 --> 00:01:35,621
takes longer to stop.

45
00:01:35,801 --> 00:01:39,301
Carbon ceramic discs resist fade much better,

46
00:01:39,301 --> 00:01:41,635
so the brakes feel the same on

47
00:01:41,635 --> 00:01:43,890
the tenth hard stop as on the

48
00:01:43,890 --> 00:01:44,357
first.

49
00:01:44,537 --> 00:01:45,646
On a race track,

50
00:01:45,646 --> 00:01:48,765
that is the difference between confidence and

51
00:01:48,765 --> 00:01:49,111
fear.

52
00:01:49,462 --> 00:01:51,528
The lower weight matters too.

53
00:01:51,708 --> 00:01:53,992
The discs are part of the unsprung

54
00:01:53,992 --> 00:01:56,814
weight, the weight that the suspension has

55
00:01:56,814 --> 00:01:58,963
to control as the wheel moves up

56
00:01:58,963 --> 00:01:59,568
and down.

57
00:01:59,748 --> 00:02:02,524
Lighter discs help the wheels follow the

58
00:02:02,524 --> 00:02:02,871
road.

59
00:02:03,051 --> 00:02:06,241
Porsche marks its ceramic brakes with yellow

60
00:02:06,241 --> 00:02:08,778
calipers, so you can spot them from

61
00:02:08,778 --> 00:02:09,576
the street.

62
00:02:09,926 --> 00:02:12,397
Why do some brake discs have holes

63
00:02:12,397 --> 00:02:13,559
drilled in them?

64
00:02:13,739 --> 00:02:14,706
In the past,

65
00:02:14,706 --> 00:02:17,766
brake pads could release gas when they

66
00:02:17,766 --> 00:02:18,813
got very hot.

67
00:02:18,993 --> 00:02:21,672
That gas formed a thin layer between

68
00:02:21,672 --> 00:02:23,234
the pad and the disc,

69
00:02:23,234 --> 00:02:24,350
like a cushion,

70
00:02:24,350 --> 00:02:25,913
and reduced the grip.

71
00:02:26,093 --> 00:02:28,114
The holes gave the gas,

72
00:02:28,114 --> 00:02:29,433
and also water,

73
00:02:29,433 --> 00:02:31,015
a place to escape.

74
00:02:31,366 --> 00:02:34,008
Modern pads release much less gas,

75
00:02:34,008 --> 00:02:35,951
but the holes still help.

76
00:02:36,131 --> 00:02:38,452
They let water escape in the rain,

77
00:02:38,452 --> 00:02:40,772
they clean the surface of the pad,

78
00:02:40,772 --> 00:02:42,819
and they save a little weight.

79
00:02:42,999 --> 00:02:45,940
Some discs use slots instead of holes,

80
00:02:45,940 --> 00:02:48,959
curved grooves that sweep the pad clean

81
00:02:48,959 --> 00:02:50,197
with every turn.

82
00:02:50,547 --> 00:02:53,182
Holes also have a downside.

83
00:02:53,362 --> 00:02:55,461
Each hole is a weak point,

84
00:02:55,461 --> 00:02:57,317
and under extreme heat,

85
00:02:57,317 --> 00:03:00,142
small cracks can start around them.

86
00:03:00,322 --> 00:03:02,760
That is why many race cars use

87
00:03:02,760 --> 00:03:05,930
slotted discs rather than drilled ones.

88
00:03:06,110 --> 00:03:07,234
On a road car,

89
00:03:07,234 --> 00:03:10,285
the holes are often there because they

90
00:03:10,285 --> 00:03:12,774
work well enough and look fast.

91
00:03:13,124 --> 00:03:16,288
Why does a supercar brake caliper have

92
00:03:16,288 --> 00:03:17,953
six or more pistons?

93
00:03:18,133 --> 00:03:20,827
The caliper is the part that squeezes

94
00:03:20,827 --> 00:03:22,719
the pads against the disc.

95
00:03:22,899 --> 00:03:26,169
A basic car has one piston pushing

96
00:03:26,169 --> 00:03:27,323
on one side.

97
00:03:27,503 --> 00:03:29,824
A big supercar pad is long,

98
00:03:29,824 --> 00:03:32,660
and one piston would push hard in

99
00:03:32,660 --> 00:03:35,583
the middle and weakly at the ends.

100
00:03:35,934 --> 00:03:39,721
Several smaller pistons spread the force evenly

101
00:03:39,721 --> 00:03:41,332
along the whole pad.

102
00:03:41,512 --> 00:03:44,064
The pad wears more evenly and grips

103
00:03:44,064 --> 00:03:45,669
with its full surface.

104
00:03:45,849 --> 00:03:48,699
The pistons can even be different sizes,

105
00:03:48,699 --> 00:03:50,907
smaller at the front of the pad

106
00:03:50,907 --> 00:03:52,546
and larger at the back,

107
00:03:52,546 --> 00:03:54,755
because the front edge of a pad

108
00:03:54,755 --> 00:03:56,251
tends to wear faster.

109
00:03:56,601 --> 00:04:00,623
High performance calipers are often made from

110
00:04:00,623 --> 00:04:03,215
one solid block of aluminium,

111
00:04:03,215 --> 00:04:04,913
called a monoblock.

112
00:04:05,093 --> 00:04:07,375
Without a joint in the middle,

113
00:04:07,375 --> 00:04:10,494
the caliper bends less under huge forces,

114
00:04:10,494 --> 00:04:13,232
so the pedal feels firm and precise.

115
00:04:13,412 --> 00:04:16,963
Less flex means the driver feels exactly

116
00:04:16,963 --> 00:04:19,449
how hard the car is braking.

117
00:04:19,799 --> 00:04:22,550
Why is a brake disc hollow inside?

118
00:04:22,730 --> 00:04:24,952
Look at the edge of most front

119
00:04:24,952 --> 00:04:27,470
discs, and you will see two plates

120
00:04:27,470 --> 00:04:29,174
with gaps between them.

121
00:04:29,354 --> 00:04:32,535
Inside are curved walls called vanes.

122
00:04:32,715 --> 00:04:34,032
As the disc spins,

123
00:04:34,032 --> 00:04:35,862
the vanes act like a fan,

124
00:04:35,862 --> 00:04:38,789
pulling air from the centre and throwing

125
00:04:38,789 --> 00:04:40,180
it out of the edge.

126
00:04:40,530 --> 00:04:43,497
That moving air carries heat away from

127
00:04:43,497 --> 00:04:45,293
the inside of the disc,

128
00:04:45,293 --> 00:04:47,635
so it cools much faster than a

129
00:04:47,635 --> 00:04:48,494
solid disc.

130
00:04:48,674 --> 00:04:51,164
Front brakes do most of the stopping

131
00:04:51,164 --> 00:04:54,208
work, because the car's weight moves forward

132
00:04:54,208 --> 00:04:55,245
when it brakes.

133
00:04:55,425 --> 00:04:57,676
That is why many cars have vented

134
00:04:57,676 --> 00:05:00,131
discs at the front and simpler solid

135
00:05:00,131 --> 00:05:01,358
discs at the back.

136
00:05:01,708 --> 00:05:03,541
On high performance discs,

137
00:05:03,541 --> 00:05:05,656
the vanes can be curved in one

138
00:05:05,656 --> 00:05:08,547
direction, which means the left and right

139
00:05:08,547 --> 00:05:10,380
discs are different parts.

140
00:05:10,560 --> 00:05:12,383
Fit them on the wrong side,

141
00:05:12,383 --> 00:05:14,542
and the fan blows the wrong way.

142
00:05:14,722 --> 00:05:17,077
It is a small reminder that even

143
00:05:17,077 --> 00:05:19,505
the inside of a disc is carefully

144
00:05:19,505 --> 00:05:20,168
designed.

145
00:05:20,518 --> 00:05:22,851
Why do many fast cars have openings

146
00:05:22,851 --> 00:05:24,985
in the front bumper that lead to

147
00:05:24,985 --> 00:05:25,719
the wheels?

148
00:05:25,899 --> 00:05:27,501
They are brake ducts.

149
00:05:27,681 --> 00:05:29,818
They catch cool air at the front

150
00:05:29,818 --> 00:05:31,889
of the car and blow it straight

151
00:05:31,889 --> 00:05:32,824
at the brakes.

152
00:05:33,004 --> 00:05:34,114
On a race track,

153
00:05:34,114 --> 00:05:36,196
the brakes can get so hot that

154
00:05:36,196 --> 00:05:38,832
the extra air makes a real difference.

155
00:05:39,183 --> 00:05:41,662
The duct sends air into the centre

156
00:05:41,662 --> 00:05:43,048
of the vented disc,

157
00:05:43,048 --> 00:05:45,382
where the vanes pull it through.

158
00:05:45,562 --> 00:05:48,395
Too little air and the brakes overheat.

159
00:05:48,575 --> 00:05:51,017
Too much air and the brakes may

160
00:05:51,017 --> 00:05:53,381
run too cold to grip properly,

161
00:05:53,381 --> 00:05:55,193
and the duct adds drag.

162
00:05:55,373 --> 00:05:57,785
Race teams change the size of the

163
00:05:57,785 --> 00:05:59,320
ducts for each track.

164
00:05:59,671 --> 00:06:00,823
On road cars,

165
00:06:00,823 --> 00:06:03,481
the ducts are usually smaller,

166
00:06:03,481 --> 00:06:05,696
but the idea is the same.

167
00:06:05,876 --> 00:06:08,649
Some cars even open and close flaps

168
00:06:08,649 --> 00:06:10,392
to change the cooling.

169
00:06:10,572 --> 00:06:12,413
The brakes are one of the most

170
00:06:12,413 --> 00:06:14,683
important things the front of the car

171
00:06:14,683 --> 00:06:15,971
has to feed with air.

172
00:06:16,321 --> 00:06:18,597
Why do brakes squeal when the pads

173
00:06:18,597 --> 00:06:19,467
are worn out?

174
00:06:19,647 --> 00:06:21,888
Often, it is on purpose.

175
00:06:22,068 --> 00:06:24,669
Many brake pads have a small metal

176
00:06:24,669 --> 00:06:26,352
tab on the back plate.

177
00:06:26,532 --> 00:06:29,008
When the pad wears down close to

178
00:06:29,008 --> 00:06:31,717
its limit, the tab touches the disc

179
00:06:31,717 --> 00:06:34,194
and makes a high pitched squeal.

180
00:06:34,374 --> 00:06:36,623
It is an alarm that does not

181
00:06:36,623 --> 00:06:38,310
need any electronics.

182
00:06:38,660 --> 00:06:41,067
If the driver ignored it and kept

183
00:06:41,067 --> 00:06:43,619
driving, the pad would wear down to

184
00:06:43,619 --> 00:06:44,932
its metal backing,

185
00:06:44,932 --> 00:06:47,485
which would grind into the disc and

186
00:06:47,485 --> 00:06:48,215
damage it.

187
00:06:48,395 --> 00:06:50,883
The squeal gives the driver time to

188
00:06:50,883 --> 00:06:53,515
replace the pads before that happens.

189
00:06:53,695 --> 00:06:57,042
Many modern cars also use an electrical

190
00:06:57,042 --> 00:07:00,046
sensor that lights a warning on the

191
00:07:00,046 --> 00:07:00,904
dashboard.

192
00:07:01,255 --> 00:07:03,983
Not every squeal means worn pads.

193
00:07:04,163 --> 00:07:07,608
Brakes can also squeal from vibration,

194
00:07:07,608 --> 00:07:09,150
dust or moisture,

195
00:07:09,150 --> 00:07:11,779
especially on a cold morning.

196
00:07:11,959 --> 00:07:14,705
But if a high pitched sound appears

197
00:07:14,705 --> 00:07:16,353
and does not go away,

198
00:07:16,353 --> 00:07:18,785
the little tab may be doing its

199
00:07:18,785 --> 00:07:19,099
job.

200
00:07:19,279 --> 00:07:21,759
It is a very cheap part that

201
00:07:21,759 --> 00:07:24,063
saves very expensive ones.

202
00:07:24,413 --> 00:07:26,244
Why is there a ring with teeth

203
00:07:26,244 --> 00:07:27,954
near each wheel of your car?

204
00:07:28,134 --> 00:07:30,141
It is part of the anti lock

205
00:07:30,141 --> 00:07:31,257
braking system.

206
00:07:31,437 --> 00:07:34,123
A sensor counts the teeth as they

207
00:07:34,123 --> 00:07:36,727
pass, and that tells the car how

208
00:07:36,727 --> 00:07:38,925
fast each wheel is turning.

209
00:07:39,105 --> 00:07:42,106
If one wheel suddenly slows much faster

210
00:07:42,106 --> 00:07:43,337
than the others,

211
00:07:43,337 --> 00:07:44,875
it is about to lock.

212
00:07:45,226 --> 00:07:48,610
A locked wheel slides instead of rolling,

213
00:07:48,610 --> 00:07:51,251
and a sliding tyre cannot steer.

214
00:07:51,431 --> 00:07:52,855
In an emergency stop,

215
00:07:52,855 --> 00:07:55,093
the car would go straight even if

216
00:07:55,093 --> 00:07:56,516
you turned the wheel.

217
00:07:56,696 --> 00:08:00,105
The anti lock system releases and reapplies

218
00:08:00,105 --> 00:08:03,038
the pressure on that wheel many times

219
00:08:03,038 --> 00:08:05,733
per second, keeping it at the edge

220
00:08:05,733 --> 00:08:06,367
of grip.

221
00:08:06,547 --> 00:08:08,259
That is the buzzing you feel in

222
00:08:08,259 --> 00:08:08,811
the pedal.

223
00:08:09,162 --> 00:08:12,519
Electronic anti lock brakes arrived on a

224
00:08:12,519 --> 00:08:15,961
production car in nineteen seventy-eight,

225
00:08:15,961 --> 00:08:17,976
on the Mercedes S Class,

226
00:08:17,976 --> 00:08:19,738
developed with Bosch.

227
00:08:19,918 --> 00:08:21,764
Today they are standard,

228
00:08:21,764 --> 00:08:24,917
and the same wheel sensors feed stability

229
00:08:24,917 --> 00:08:28,070
control, which can brake single wheels to

230
00:08:28,070 --> 00:08:30,147
keep the car from spinning.

231
00:08:30,327 --> 00:08:32,770
A ring of teeth became the base

232
00:08:32,770 --> 00:08:34,425
of modern car safety.

233
00:08:34,775 --> 00:08:36,960
Why does brake fluid need to be

234
00:08:36,960 --> 00:08:38,652
changed every few years?

235
00:08:38,832 --> 00:08:41,052
The fluid carries the force of your

236
00:08:41,052 --> 00:08:42,257
foot to the brakes.

237
00:08:42,437 --> 00:08:45,011
It can do that because liquids do

238
00:08:45,011 --> 00:08:46,025
not compress.

239
00:08:46,205 --> 00:08:49,270
But brake fluid slowly absorbs water from

240
00:08:49,270 --> 00:08:52,037
the air, through the hoses and seals,

241
00:08:52,037 --> 00:08:54,355
even when the system is closed.

242
00:08:54,705 --> 00:08:57,622
Water lowers the boiling point of the

243
00:08:57,622 --> 00:08:58,095
fluid.

244
00:08:58,275 --> 00:09:00,904
Fresh fluid boils at well over two

245
00:09:00,904 --> 00:09:03,920
hundred degrees, but with some water in

246
00:09:03,920 --> 00:09:06,394
it, the boiling point can drop a

247
00:09:06,394 --> 00:09:06,704
lot.

248
00:09:06,884 --> 00:09:08,325
During hard braking,

249
00:09:08,325 --> 00:09:10,993
the heat from the brakes travels into

250
00:09:10,993 --> 00:09:11,713
the fluid.

251
00:09:11,893 --> 00:09:12,962
If it boils,

252
00:09:12,962 --> 00:09:14,833
it forms gas bubbles,

253
00:09:14,833 --> 00:09:16,793
and gas does compress.

254
00:09:17,143 --> 00:09:19,863
The result is a pedal that suddenly

255
00:09:19,863 --> 00:09:22,816
goes soft and sinks towards the floor,

256
00:09:22,816 --> 00:09:25,769
exactly when you need the brakes most.

257
00:09:25,949 --> 00:09:28,991
That is why car makers recommend changing

258
00:09:28,991 --> 00:09:30,772
the fluid on a schedule,

259
00:09:30,772 --> 00:09:32,404
often every two years.

260
00:09:32,584 --> 00:09:33,714
A cheap liquid,

261
00:09:33,714 --> 00:09:37,253
quietly protecting the most important system in

262
00:09:37,253 --> 00:09:37,855
the car.

263
00:09:38,205 --> 00:09:40,907
Why do Formula One brakes glow red

264
00:09:40,907 --> 00:09:42,814
when the car slows down?

265
00:09:42,994 --> 00:09:46,073
Because they are made of carbon carbon,

266
00:09:46,073 --> 00:09:48,757
a material that works best when it

267
00:09:48,757 --> 00:09:50,099
is extremely hot.

268
00:09:50,279 --> 00:09:52,901
The discs can reach around one thousand

269
00:09:52,901 --> 00:09:54,784
degrees during hard braking.

270
00:09:54,964 --> 00:09:56,365
At that temperature,

271
00:09:56,365 --> 00:09:58,467
they shine like the coils in a

272
00:09:58,467 --> 00:09:59,027
toaster.

273
00:09:59,378 --> 00:10:02,176
Carbon carbon brakes have a strange problem.

274
00:10:02,356 --> 00:10:03,808
When they are cold,

275
00:10:03,808 --> 00:10:05,107
they barely grip.

276
00:10:05,287 --> 00:10:07,346
Drivers have to warm them up,

277
00:10:07,346 --> 00:10:09,688
and the cars use ducts and covers

278
00:10:09,688 --> 00:10:12,314
to keep them in the right temperature

279
00:10:12,314 --> 00:10:12,811
window.

280
00:10:12,991 --> 00:10:15,841
Each disc is drilled with hundreds of

281
00:10:15,841 --> 00:10:18,152
tiny holes to cool it from the

282
00:10:18,152 --> 00:10:18,691
inside.

283
00:10:19,041 --> 00:10:20,290
When they are hot,

284
00:10:20,290 --> 00:10:22,164
the deceleration is brutal.

285
00:10:22,344 --> 00:10:24,825
A Formula One car can slow down

286
00:10:24,825 --> 00:10:27,466
with a force of around five times

287
00:10:27,466 --> 00:10:28,667
its own weight,

288
00:10:28,667 --> 00:10:31,388
pushing the driver into the belts.

289
00:10:31,568 --> 00:10:34,590
It is why carbon ceramic road brakes

290
00:10:34,590 --> 00:10:37,361
exist, a version of the same idea

291
00:10:37,361 --> 00:10:39,544
that also works when cold.

292
00:10:39,894 --> 00:10:43,023
Why do electric cars barely use their

293
00:10:43,023 --> 00:10:44,038
brake discs?

294
00:10:44,218 --> 00:10:46,990
Because the electric motor can work in

295
00:10:46,990 --> 00:10:47,574
reverse.

296
00:10:47,754 --> 00:10:49,281
When you lift your foot,

297
00:10:49,281 --> 00:10:51,190
the motor becomes a generator.

298
00:10:51,370 --> 00:10:52,591
The wheels turn it,

299
00:10:52,591 --> 00:10:55,096
and it makes electricity that goes back

300
00:10:55,096 --> 00:10:56,188
into the battery.

301
00:10:56,368 --> 00:10:59,101
That resistance slows the car down,

302
00:10:59,101 --> 00:11:01,210
without touching the discs.

303
00:11:01,560 --> 00:11:04,776
Instead of turning the car's speed into

304
00:11:04,776 --> 00:11:08,653
wasted heat, regenerative braking turns part of

305
00:11:08,653 --> 00:11:10,302
it back into energy.

306
00:11:10,482 --> 00:11:11,829
In city traffic,

307
00:11:11,829 --> 00:11:13,176
with many stops,

308
00:11:13,176 --> 00:11:15,532
this can add a lot of range.

309
00:11:15,712 --> 00:11:18,730
Many drivers learn to drive with one

310
00:11:18,730 --> 00:11:21,999
pedal, slowing down just by lifting off

311
00:11:21,999 --> 00:11:23,340
the accelerator.

312
00:11:23,690 --> 00:11:25,436
The irony is that the brakes can

313
00:11:25,436 --> 00:11:27,509
now suffer from being used too little.

314
00:11:27,689 --> 00:11:30,081
Iron discs that rarely get hot can

315
00:11:30,081 --> 00:11:32,542
rust and lose their smooth surface.

316
00:11:32,722 --> 00:11:35,415
Some electric cars use the brakes on

317
00:11:35,415 --> 00:11:37,359
purpose from time to time,

318
00:11:37,359 --> 00:11:39,154
just to keep them clean.

319
00:11:39,334 --> 00:11:41,736
After a century of fighting heat,

320
00:11:41,736 --> 00:11:44,575
brakes now sometimes need a little more

321
00:11:44,575 --> 00:11:45,012
of it.

322
00:11:45,362 --> 00:11:46,674
So here are the ten.

323
00:11:46,854 --> 00:11:49,903
Ceramic discs that resist heat and weigh

324
00:11:49,903 --> 00:11:50,894
half as much.

325
00:11:51,074 --> 00:11:53,947
Holes and slots that let gas and

326
00:11:53,947 --> 00:11:55,114
water escape.

327
00:11:55,294 --> 00:11:57,547
Many pistons for even pressure.

328
00:11:57,727 --> 00:12:00,258
Hollow discs with fans inside.

329
00:12:00,438 --> 00:12:02,295
Ducts that feed them air.

330
00:12:02,475 --> 00:12:05,373
A tab that squeals when the pad

331
00:12:05,373 --> 00:12:06,121
is worn.

332
00:12:06,301 --> 00:12:09,031
A toothed ring that stops wheels from

333
00:12:09,031 --> 00:12:09,621
locking.

334
00:12:09,801 --> 00:12:11,833
Fluid that must stay dry.

335
00:12:12,013 --> 00:12:13,592
Carbon brakes that glow.

336
00:12:13,772 --> 00:12:16,244
And motors that brake by charging the

337
00:12:16,244 --> 00:12:16,779
battery.

338
00:12:17,129 --> 00:12:18,975
Speed is easy.

339
00:12:19,155 --> 00:12:21,651
Stopping is the real engineering.

